nters a variable  where this variable is defined and what  its scope is  there are three scopes local global they  sort of speak for themselves  and then you have free which is that  it's  declared somewhere in an enclosing scope  that's  especially true for things like closures  so before we actually compile our uh  our statements into bytecode we  construct a symbolic table  and in type languages for instance like  c this is the place where you would  actually  check hey is the variable declared does  it have the right type do you return the  right type  and if not it will give you a bunch of  errors well python does not do that but  it needs to know  the symbolic table for this scoping  because the scopes are  important in order to create the right  instructions  and then another challenge we have is  control flow  so if we were to just go statement by  statement out of the ast  we'll run into a bit of an issue that if  we evaluate this if statement  um we'll see the sort of spaghetti code  in action  if we run this if statement um we we're  going to print r so we're going to skip  a part  but if we are compiling we don't know  where to skip to just yet because we're  not there  we don't know where it's going to jump  to because we're  compiling still and we haven't seen that  part  and this is especially true for all  things that change control flow  um we need to know where to jump to and  we need to know  how to create the right byte code for  that  so how python approaches this is with  something called the control flow graph  so it splits up the all the instructions  you have  into basic blocks and these basic blocks  they have  one entry point and can have multiple  exit points  so here we split up our our stuff in in  basic blocks  we have num equals one plus two we have  our again if  if this is true print even if it's false  we'll print out and then we  return none that's uh in this case  python always returns something  so return none is added  and the 